   260	**87% of cited sentences say what their cited lines say.** The grounding check only proves that a
   261	citation points at lines the job retrieved. This eval asks the next question: does the sentence match
   262	those lines? It ran on the zustand wiki that Haiku 5.5 wrote (21 pages, 286 cited sentences).
   263	
   264	**Method.** `pnpm eval:citations` splits every page into sentences and keeps each one that cites a
   265	source. It reads the cited lines at the cited commit. Two judges from different vendors, Claude Sonnet
   266	5.5 and GPT-5.5, then label each pair `supported`, `partial` or `unsupported`. Each judge must quote
   267	the line that supports the claim, and a quote that is not in the lines is flagged. Neither judge is
   268	the author model.
   269	
   270	**Checking the judges without human labels.** A judge that says "supported" too easily would make the
   271	wiki look better than it is. To measure that, the eval adds 68 planted pairs: real sentences with their
   272	citation swapped for other lines, 33 from the same file and 35 from another file. Their answer is known
   273	in advance: the new lines should not support the sentence.
   274	
   275	| | Claude Sonnet 5.5 | GPT-5.5 |
   276	| --- | --- | --- |
   277	| supported | 85.7% | 82.3% |
   278	| partial | 12.5% | 16.5% |
   279	| unsupported | 1.9% | 1.2% |
   280	| planted wrong citations rejected | 54/58 (93%) | 57/60 (95%) |
   281	| cost | $1.17 | $3.31 |
   282	
   283	The judges agree on 215 of the 230 sentences they both judged (93.5%, Cohen's kappa 0.76, where 1 is
   284	perfect agreement and 0 is chance). Where they agree: 187 supported (87.0%), 25 partial, 3 unsupported.
   285	
   286	**What the runs showed:**
   287	
   288	- **The judges were not lenient.** Every planted pair a judge accepted was read by hand, and in all
   289	  five the swapped lines really did support the sentence. The docs and types repeat the same fact
   290	  in several places, so a same-file decoy can be right by chance. No judge accepted a decoy that was
   291	  actually wrong.
   292	- **All three rejected sentences are true, but cite the wrong lines.** `useCallback` is on line 32 of
   293	  `react.ts`; the page cites line 31. The grounding check accepts any range inside a retrieved chunk,
   294	  so the model can narrow a chunk to the wrong lines and still pass.
   295	- **The 25 agreed `partial` sentences are three kinds,** read by hand:
   296	  - **11 factual slips** (about 5% of the agreed sentences). Four write `create<T>(...)` where
   297	    zustand's TypeScript form is the curried `create<T>()(...)`. Others write `getState.count` for
   298	    `getState().count`, say "afterwards" where the docs say "before", or claim a custom `merge` keeps
   299	    a change that the cited test shows being overwritten.
   300	  - **7 ranges cut too short:** the sentence describes a function's body, and the citation stops at
   301	    its signature. The same near miss as the rejected three.
   302	  - **7 overreaches in wording or context,** such as calling listed middlewares "community" ones, or
   303	    "This matches `useState`" with no clear subject.
   304	- **GPT-5.5 is stricter about wording**, and cost 3× as much. Most of the 15 disputes are GPT marking
   305	  `partial` for phrases like "the official docs". Sonnet alone plus planted pairs is enough for a
   306	  routine run.
   307	
   308	**Coverage.** The run stopped at the OpenRouter key's spend limit with 631 of 708 verdicts made. 56
   309	sentences have one verdict or none, so the 87% is over the 215 agreed sentences. If every missing one
   310	had been agreed and unsupported, the rate would be 69%; the trend in the judged ones makes that very
   311	unlikely. Two sentences were judged with garbled text, from a link-stripping bug fixed after the run.
